ield/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bc35b8b-2ef7-5beb-b61a-6e40047f0bfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367763} {:answer/id #uuid "a8a96672-6012-580f-8171-180174e0e4a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bc35b8b-2ef7-5beb-b61a-6e40047f0bfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367763} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0e961ee-a2d0-5afc-b3e7-0cc9812dbe86" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ed2e684-f57d-5c2f-88f3-95cfac441d13" :question/math-academy-id "q-333843" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ -3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{t} \\\\ 6{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & -2{e}^{t} \\\\ -3{e}^{t} & 6{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({e}^{t})\\cdot (6{e}^{t})-(-2{e}^{t})\\cdot (-3{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =6{e}^{2t}-6{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367765 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee461d3e-3afa-51c6-bddd-355668cac3ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Vector-Valued Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91e8d3bd-b503-5ffd-a534-aa87a8d3c775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367767} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb07331-b362-5e63-961d-1f65bc363414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367768} {:answer/id #uuid "c62ea812-bfec-53a6-9a2b-dea6b7ddc7f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367769} {:answer/id #uuid "e9276cbc-28bf-55b2-afc6-77a30c34e669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91e8d3bd-b503-5ffd-a534-aa87a8d3c775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367767} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e92668a-2391-5bc1-ac22-193f588c983f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367766} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce79c784-1e4e-5fd7-9e75-c3ae47ca144a" :answer